# JAVA CODE INTELLIGENCE — BACKEND ONLY
# Java 25 + Spring Boot + JavaParser + JGraphT + SQLite

## 1. ROLE

Act as a Principal Java Architect, Static Analysis Expert, Graph Algorithms Engineer, and Performance Optimization Specialist.

Design and implement a complete, efficient, maintainable Java backend capable of analyzing an existing Java project and building a persistent method-level and class-level dependency graph.

The backend will later be consumed by a React application using Mermaid.js and Monaco Editor.

IMPORTANT:
- Implement BACKEND ONLY.
- Do not create any frontend code.
- Do not create React components.
- Do not generate Mermaid diagrams.
- Expose structured JSON APIs suitable for future graph visualization.

Work autonomously and implement actual working functionality.

---

# 2. ACTUAL PROJECT SIZE

The target Java project contains approximately:

- 1,790 classes and interfaces.
- 22,000 methods.
- 10 lines of code per method on average.
- 220,000 lines inside method bodies.

Optimize for this workload.

Do not overengineer for millions of methods.

The architecture should nevertheless remain scalable.

The application must support:

- Multi-module Maven projects.
- Java 8 through Java 25 source code.
- Interfaces and inheritance.
- Method overloading.
- Method overriding.
- Generic types.
- Spring dependency injection.
- External Maven dependencies.
- Complex method-call chains.

---

# 3. TECHNOLOGY STACK

Mandatory:

- Java JDK 25
- Spring Boot compatible with Java 25
- Maven
- JavaParser
- JavaSymbolSolver
- JGraphT
- SQLite
- Spring JDBC
- Caffeine Cache
- Jackson
- SLF4J

Optional:

- SootUp for advanced bytecode analysis if necessary.

Avoid unnecessary frameworks.

Do not introduce:

- Neo4j
- Docker
- Redis
- Elasticsearch
- Kafka
- Microservices

The application must run as a single Spring Boot process.

---

# 4. BACKEND ARCHITECTURE

Use a modular monolith.

Prefer package-level modularity over unnecessary Maven modules.

Suggested structure:

src/main/java/com/codeintelligence/
    application/
    analysis/
        parser/
        resolver/
        extractor/
        incremental/
    graph/
        model/
        builder/
        traversal/
        metrics/
    architecture/
        modules/
        dependencies/
        communities/
        cycles/
    source/
        reader/
        locator/
        navigation/
    persistence/
        entity/
        repository/
    api/
        controller/
        dto/
    config/

Maintain clear separation between:

1. Source parsing.
2. Symbol resolution.
3. Graph construction.
4. Graph persistence.
5. Graph algorithms.
6. Architecture analysis.
7. Source navigation.
8. REST API.

Avoid circular dependencies.

Use Java records for immutable DTOs.

Avoid unnecessary interfaces and abstractions.

---

# 5. PROJECT IMPORT

Implement the ability to register an existing local Java project.

Input:

{
  "name": "legacy-project",
  "rootPath": "C:/projects/legacy"
}

The backend must:

1. Validate the project directory.
2. Detect Maven modules.
3. Detect Java source directories.
4. Detect source files.
5. Resolve available Maven dependencies.
6. Build the project structure.
7. Persist project metadata.

Support Windows and Linux paths.

Never modify the analyzed project's source files.

The analyzer must be read-only.

---

# 6. JAVA SOURCE ANALYSIS

Use JavaParser and JavaSymbolSolver.

Extract the following entities.

## MODULE

- ID
- Name
- Path
- Parent module
- Maven coordinates
- Dependencies

## PACKAGE

- ID
- Name
- Module
- Classes count

## CLASS / INTERFACE

- ID
- Fully qualified name
- Simple name
- Module
- Package
- Source file
- Start/end lines
- Visibility
- Modifiers
- Annotations
- Parent class
- Implemented interfaces
- Fields
- Constructors
- Methods

Support:

- Classes
- Interfaces
- Enums
- Records
- Abstract classes
- Nested classes
- Anonymous classes

## METHOD

Extract:

- Stable unique ID
- Declaring class
- Name
- Fully qualified signature
- Return type
- Parameters
- Parameter names
- Parameter types
- Generic parameters
- Exceptions
- Annotations
- Visibility
- Static/abstract/final modifiers
- Start line
- End line
- Start column
- End column
- Source file
- Incoming call count
- Outgoing call count

Use unique identifiers that distinguish overloaded methods.

Include module identity where necessary.

---

# 7. METHOD CALL ANALYSIS

This is a critical feature.

Build a complete static method-call graph.

Detect:

- Direct method calls
- Static method calls
- Constructor calls
- Interface method calls
- Inherited methods
- Overridden methods
- Polymorphic calls
- Lambda expressions
- Method references
- Recursive calls
- Cross-module calls

Support Spring patterns:

- @Controller
- @RestController
- @Service
- @Repository
- @Component
- @Autowired
- Constructor injection
- @Bean
- @Configuration
- @Qualifier
- @Primary

When a method invokes an interface method:

1. Identify the declared interface method.
2. Find compatible implementations.
3. Use Spring injection metadata where possible.
4. Identify possible runtime targets.
5. Create relationships with resolution status.

Resolution statuses:

EXACT
POSSIBLE_DYNAMIC
FRAMEWORK_INFERRED
UNRESOLVED

Never silently discard unresolved calls.

Never represent an inferred call as a guaranteed runtime call.

Store the source location of every invocation.

Example:

OrderController.getOrders()
    |
    v
OrderService.findOrders()
    |
    +--> OrderValidator.validate()
    |
    +--> OrderRepository.findAll()

Store individual call sites separately from aggregated
method-to-method relationships.

---

# 8. GRAPH DATA MODEL

Use JGraphT.

Implement a directed graph with:

## Nodes

- PROJECT
- MODULE
- PACKAGE
- CLASS
- INTERFACE
- METHOD
- CONSTRUCTOR
- FIELD
- REST_ENDPOINT
- DATABASE_TABLE
- EXTERNAL_DEPENDENCY

## Relationships

- CONTAINS
- DECLARES
- EXTENDS
- IMPLEMENTS
- CALLS
- OVERRIDES
- USES
- READS
- WRITES
- INJECTS
- DEPENDS_ON
- EXPOSES
- QUERIES
- UPDATES

Use a property graph abstraction.

Each node must have:

- Stable ID
- Type
- Name
- Qualified name
- Module ID
- Metadata

Each relationship must have:

- Source ID
- Target ID
- Relationship type
- Resolution status
- Call-site information where applicable

Optimize the in-memory graph for fast traversal.

Avoid duplicating large metadata objects inside graph vertices.

Use lightweight IDs where practical.

---

# 9. SQLITE PERSISTENCE

Use SQLite as the persistent storage engine.

Create tables:

projects
modules
packages
types
methods
parameters
fields
source_files
graph_nodes
graph_edges
call_sites
analysis_runs
class_metrics
method_metrics
communities

Create indexes for:

- Qualified class name
- Qualified method signature
- Module ID
- Source node ID
- Target node ID
- Relationship type
- Source file ID

Enable WAL mode.

Use prepared statements and batch inserts.

Use transactions.

Ensure consistent graph updates.

Avoid N+1 queries.

Store source locations rather than duplicating entire
Java source files in SQLite.

Implement database schema initialization and migrations.

---

# 10. GRAPH LOADING STRATEGY

The target project contains approximately 22,000 methods.

Prefer keeping the method-call graph in memory.

Use SQLite for persistence.

On application startup:

1. Load registered project metadata.
2. Load graph vertices and edges on demand.
3. Build or restore the in-memory graph when required.
4. Cache frequently used graph queries.

Support multiple registered projects.

Avoid loading all projects into memory unnecessarily.

Use bounded caches.

The graph must support fast:

- Caller lookup
- Callee lookup
- Shortest path
- Reachability
- Cycle detection
- Impact analysis

---

# 11. GRAPH TRAVERSAL

Implement the following operations.

## Direct callers

Given method M, return all methods calling M.

## Direct callees

Given method M, return all methods invoked by M.

## Transitive callers

Return all methods that can reach M.

## Transitive callees

Return all methods reachable from M.

## Shortest path

Find the shortest method-call path between two methods.

## Multiple paths

Find multiple distinct paths between two methods.

Use configurable limits to avoid combinatorial explosion.

## Forward traversal

Explore outgoing calls.

## Reverse traversal

Explore incoming calls.

## Bidirectional traversal

Explore both directions.

Parameters:

- methodId
- direction
- maxDepth
- maxNodes
- maxEdges
- relationshipTypes
- includeExternalDependencies
- includeUnresolved

Implement cycle protection.

Support cancellation and timeouts for expensive operations.

---

# 12. CLASS AND METHOD METRICS

Calculate metrics at method, class, package and module levels.

## Dependency metrics

- Incoming relationships
- Outgoing relationships
- Fan-in
- Fan-out
- Number of callers
- Number of callees
- Number of dependent classes
- Number of dependencies
- Cross-module dependencies

## Graph metrics

- PageRank
- Betweenness centrality
- Strongly connected components
- Reachability
- Cycle participation

## Code metrics

- Lines of code
- Method count
- Cyclomatic complexity
- Coupling indicators
- Cohesion indicators

## Impact analysis

For each class or method calculate:

- Directly affected methods
- Transitively affected methods
- Affected classes
- Affected modules
- Affected REST endpoints

Use scalable algorithms.

Do not run expensive exact centrality algorithms unnecessarily.

Use approximation when appropriate.

---

# 13. CLASS RELEVANCE SCORE

Implement configurable class relevance scoring.

Default formula:

RelevanceScore =
    0.25 * PageRank
  + 0.20 * FanIn
  + 0.20 * Betweenness
  + 0.20 * Impact
  + 0.15 * CrossModuleDependencies

Normalize metrics before combining them.

Make weights configurable.

Return:

- Global relevance score
- Individual metric values
- Ranking within project
- Ranking within module
- Number of incoming relationships
- Number of outgoing relationships
- Impact indicators

Do not assume highly connected technical utilities are
necessarily business-critical.

Keep structural relevance separate from inferred
business importance.

---

# 14. AUTOMATIC MODULE DETECTION

Detect physical and logical modules.

## Physical modules

- Maven modules
- Packages
- Source boundaries

## Logical modules

Infer candidate business modules using:

- Class dependencies
- Method-call relationships
- Package structure
- Shared entities
- Domain vocabulary
- Service interactions
- Repository relationships
- Graph communities

Implement:

- Strongly connected components
- Weakly connected components
- Community detection
- Dependency clustering

Use an appropriate community detection algorithm.

For each detected module return:

- Module ID
- Suggested name
- Classes
- Methods count
- Internal relationships
- External relationships
- Coupling metrics
- Cohesion indicators
- Detection evidence

Clearly distinguish physical modules from inferred logical modules.

---

# 15. SOURCE CODE NAVIGATION

This feature is mandatory.

The future React frontend must be able to display the exact
source code of a method when its graph node is selected.

Implement source retrieval APIs.

For each method store:

- Project ID
- Module ID
- Source file path
- File hash
- Start line
- End line
- Start column
- End column
- Method signature

Implement two retrieval modes.

## Method source

Return only the selected method source.

## Complete class source

Return the complete Java source file with the selected
method location.

Example response:

{
  "methodId": "m123",
  "className": "OrderService",
  "methodName": "findOrders",
  "filePath": "src/main/java/com/app/OrderService.java",
  "startLine": 42,
  "endLine": 51,
  "source": "public List<Order> findOrders(...) { ... }",
  "parameters": [],
  "returnType": "List<Order>"
}

Read source directly from the filesystem.

Detect stale source locations.

Never return incorrect source silently.

Restrict file access to registered project roots.

Support external source JARs when available.

---

# 16. CALL-SITE NAVIGATION

Implement precise navigation between methods.

When the future Monaco Editor sends a source position:

1. Identify the corresponding indexed call site.
2. Resolve its target method.
3. Return target method metadata.
4. Return possible implementations when ambiguous.
5. Return target source location.

Example:

GET /api/source/resolve-call?fileId=123&line=48&column=15

Response:

{
  "callSiteId": "c456",
  "resolution": "EXACT",
  "targets": [
    {
      "methodId": "m789",
      "className": "OrderRepository",
      "methodName": "findAll",
      "filePath": "...",
      "startLine": 20
    }
  ]
}

Do not use text matching to resolve method calls.

Use JavaParser symbol resolution and indexed call-site data.

---

